  "scenario_metadata": {
    "product": "Automotive-grade microcontrollers (MCUs)",
    "period_unit": "week",
    "horizon_periods": 10,
    "notes": "Illustrative synthetic scenario data. Tariff baseline/spike figures are grounded in real 2025-2026 Section 301 semiconductor tariff policy; all other figures are invented for modeling purposes. Demand/capacity/inventory quantities are scaled into hundreds/low-thousands; per-unit rates are left unchanged. Demand noise is uniform +/-75%. Overseas Manufacturer lead time is 2 weeks. Player supplier allocation uses soft suggested shares (and a soft 50% single-supplier ceiling); hard diversification caps are reserved for the Oracle/MILP."
  },
  "demand_generation_methodology": {
    "description": "Both historical_demand_last_8_weeks and actual_demand_ground_truth_by_week are generated the same way: weekly_demand (the fixed baseline) multiplied by a noise factor drawn from a uniform distribution on [0.25, 1.75] (i.e. +/-75%), rounded to a whole unit. The demand-spike disruption (week 6, C5, x1.5) is layered multiplicatively on top of this noise for that specific customer-week only. Generated ONCE via a seeded random process (seed 20260810), then frozen -- identical for every participant and every forecasting method. Actual demand is intentionally generated independently of any forecasting method."
  },
